.Dd September 1, 2026
.Dt BSDEXTENSION 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDExtension
.Nd system-extension (kernel module) broker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon owns loading of kernel
.Em system extensions
(kernel modules), a duty removed from
.Xr init 8
and
.Xr capsule 8 .
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.SystemExtension
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
A client asks
.Nm
either to ensure a named extension is loaded or to query whether one is
currently loaded without attempting a load; the discovery
.Em domain
layer resolves
.Dq Li system.SystemExtension
only for SYSTEM-domain clients, so an ordinary user service can never reach
.Nm
and therefore can never cause kernel code to load.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr switchboard 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Ss Operation
During startup,
.Nm
declares the
.Dq kldload
system-capability gate in its manifest and
.Xr switchboard 8
mints the matching
.Dq system
token and delivers it as a bootstrap capability.
.Nm
holds no
.Pa /dev/mac_capability
handle of its own \(em capsule (PID 1) owns that device \(em and no socket
appears anywhere in the path;
.Fn service_provider_authorize_capabilities
adds
.Nm Ns 's
process nonce to the gate's authorized set, and it holds that authority for
its entire lifetime.
It then exposes its service name and dispatches each
accepted client on its own thread.
The broker is born in capability mode as the unprivileged capability user:
it performs each
.Xr kldload 2
.Em through
the held gate token in kernel context, so the raw privileged syscall stays
refused inside the Capsicum cage and the sandbox is never loosened.
Because a worker thread shares the daemon's held gate token, its
.Xr kldload 2
passes the gate under the standing claim; no access token is minted or
delivered, and no socket appears anywhere in the path.
.Pp
A request names a single kernel module as a safe filename component: a name
containing a slash, or equal to
.Dq \&.
or
.Dq \&.. ,
is rejected.
For a load request the module is loaded from the kernel module path with
.Xr kldload 2 ;
a module that is already present is reported as success, so the load path never
needs to query and uses only the
.Dq kldload
gate.
A query request instead reports whether the module is loaded, using
.Xr kldfind 2 ,
a read-only query; module enumeration is ungated, so no capability is
needed for it.
Both operations are subject to the same allow-list: a client may query only a
module it could load, so a name that is not allow-listed is refused rather than
answered, and a query leaks nothing about modules a client may not load.
There is deliberately no unload operation; safe removal would require
per-consumer module ownership that
.Nm
does not track.
.Pp
A LIST request enumerates the module allow-list, returning each permitted
module name and the count.
The allow-list is global rather than per-label, so every caller sees the same
set; the reply carries the whole list in one message and loads nothing.
.Pp
A RELOAD request requires ADMIN rights on the accepted service channel.
It rereads only the daemon's configured policy path; clients cannot supply
a path or policy contents.
The replacement must be a regular file owned by the daemon's effective UID,
not writable by group or others, with an explicit allowed_extensions array.
An empty array denies all modules.
Missing or invalid replacements leave the last active policy intact.
Updates are published atomically to all workers, including existing sessions.
Requests already admitted under the previous policy may finish.
Reload does not unload modules or restart the core service.
.Pp
.Nm
is a reconcile client of the capability container model
.Pq Pa docs/capability-container-model.md :
every module it loads on a bundle's behalf is attributed to that bundle
from the kernel-stamped container identity of the requesting unit, never
from the wire, in a per-boot owner map.
A forked reconcile child compares the map against the installed or running
bundles read from the delivered
.Pa /Capabilities/System ,
.Pa /Capabilities/Apps
and
.Pa /Capabilities/Run/live
directories: once at boot, then on a timer, unloading a bundle's module only
after it has been seen gone on two consecutive passes.
Only a module
.Nm
itself loaded is ever unloaded, and only when no other bundle still claims it;
a module found already loaded is attributed for the record but left alone,
and a module the kernel reports busy stays loaded and is retried on the next
pass.
The map is reset whenever the boot epoch changes, since modules do not
survive a reboot.
Reclaim is soft: without the map or the delivered directories,
.Nm
serves loads exactly as before.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width BSDEXTENSION_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
.It Ev BSDEXTENSION_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
Seconds between the timer passes of the module reconcile, and therefore the
grace window an uninstalled bundle's modules are given before they are
unloaded.
Accepted range 10 to 86400; the default is 300.
Set through the unit's
.Ic environment
block.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap" -compact
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/SystemExtension.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.It Pa /Capabilities/Config/bsdextension.ucl
Optional allow-list policy, read at daemon startup and on authorized reload.
The allowed_extensions array replaces the built-in list.
Use
.Xr sysextctl 8
to reload after atomically replacing the file.
.It Pa /var/run/BSDExtension/modules.meta
The per-boot module to bundle owner map the reconcile works from.
.El
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The
.Dq BSDExtension
provider exports
.Sy list ,
which fires when a LIST request is answered with the client label, the number
of allow-listed modules reported, and the result.
The result argument is zero on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value on failure.
.Pp
.Sy reclaim_pass
fires after every reconcile pass with when (0 boot, 1 timer) and the pass's
live, owned, orphaned, destroyed and failed counts.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kldload 2 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr capsule 8 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr sysextctl 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon appeared in
.Fx 5BSD .
It takes over the kernel-module loading duty formerly performed inside the
.Xr capsule 8
as part of moving privileged work out of PID 1 and behind named,
domain-restricted, socket-free services.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Kory Heard
